; forms.psol -- three forms a module declares for itself.
;
; A form is not a method, and the difference is the whole reason it exists: its
; arguments arrive unevaluated, so the template may put them somewhere the
; caller never wrote. `unless(x > #5, y:print)` passes `y:print` as *code*, and
; the block around it is the template's doing.
;
; Everything below holds for this file. A module that wants none of it declares
; none of it and reads exactly as Solveig does.

@infix  +   60 add.
@infix  <   40 lessThan.
@infix  >   40 greaterThan.

; The argument is not evaluated before it arrives, which is what lets the
; braces be the template's business rather than the caller's.
@syntax unless(test, body) => test:not:ifTrue({ body }).

; Both arguments become blocks. Written as a method this would need two sets of
; braces at every call, and every call would be a chance to forget one.
@syntax while(test, body) => { test }:whileTrue({ body }).

; This one needs somewhere to put a value, and that is where hygiene starts
; mattering rather than being a word.
@syntax swap(a, b) => { | t | t := a. a := b. b := t }:value.

; And this one reaches *out* -- `total` is not a parameter, so it means the
; global of that name, whatever the caller happens to have called things.
@syntax bump(n) => total := total:add(n).

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

x := #3.
unless(x > #5, "three is not more than five":print).

; A group is how a form takes more than one statement, since a `.` would
; otherwise end the argument.
i := #1.
sum := #0.
while(i < #5, (sum := sum + i. i := i + #1)).
sum:print.                        ; #10

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The form binds `t`. So does the caller. Nothing happens.
;
; This is the example that proves itself rather than asserting itself: an
; unhygienic expansion of `swap` compiles, runs, and prints #1 and #2 -- the
; values unswapped, because the template's `t := a` wrote over the caller's `t`
; before `a := b` could read it. Read the generated .sol beside this file to see
; the name the expansion actually used.

t := #1.
u := #2.
swap(t, u).
t:print.                          ; #2
u:print.                          ; #1

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The form reaches out for `total`. The caller has a local called `total`.
; Nothing happens, in the other direction.
;
; This one proves itself too, and it is the more alarming of the two: an
; unprotected expansion prints #105 and #0 -- the form updated the caller's
; temporary and the global it meant was never touched. Both numbers are wrong
; and neither is an error.

total := #0.

run := { | total |
    total := #100.
    bump(#5).
    total }.

run:value:print.                  ; #100 -- the caller's local, untouched
total:print.                      ; #5   -- the global, which is what bump meant
